  [1mMy call on each item:[0m

  [1m1. Strict comparison — done.[0m Real bug, verified by the repo's own failing test.

  [1m2. Keep [38;5;153mtime.monotonic()[39m — declined.[0m Wall-clock is unsafe for interval arithmetic. An NTP step backwards leaves [38;5;153mcutoff
[39m  behind the stored timestamps, so nothing evicts and the window stalls, blocking traffic longer than configured. A
  step forwards drains the deque in one pass and releases a full burst. Both are silent, both are unreproducible in
  tests, and both are worse than the problem being solved. The reviewer's actual need — correlating limiter decisions
  with log timestamps — doesn't require changing the window clock; it's solved at the logging call site by recording
  [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m there, where a clock jump costs you a slightly-off log line instead of a broken rate limiter.

  I didn't add that wall-clock capture, because this repo has no logging at all — no call site to correlate with. Adding
  a second timestamp with no consumer would be the same speculative work I'm rejecting in item 3, and I'd rather be
  consistent than split the difference. When the gateway's logging lands here, that's the moment to wire it, and it's a
  small change.

  [1m3. Storage-backend interface — declined.[0m Nothing in the repo references Redis or any alternative backend, and
  [38;5;153mpyproject.toml[39m has zero runtime dependencies. An ABC with one implementor is cost now for no benefit, but the stronger
  reason is that it would be the [3mwrong[0m interface: this limiter evicts then appends as two steps, which is fine for an
  in-process deque and racy across processes against Redis. A correct Redis backend needs that collapsed into one atomic
  server-side operation — a Lua script, or [38;5;153mZREMRANGEBYSCORE[39m + [38;5;153mZADD[39m + [38;5;153mZCARD[39m in a MULTI. An interface shaped around the
  deque would bake in a non-atomic contract that the real backend has to work around, and we'd rewrite it anyway. Better
  to write it when the atomicity requirement is in front of us.

  If Redis is genuinely scheduled, say so and I'll design the interface around atomic check-and-append instead.

